# 08 — Structs & typedef

## What is this?
A `struct` (structure) groups related variables of different types into a single user-defined aggregate data type. `typedef` creates a convenient type alias.

## Why does C have it?
- Structs provide the foundation for modeling domain entities, records, and complex data structures (points, users, nodes).
- Access fields directly via the dot operator `.` or via pointers with arrow operator `->`.

## Syntax and Core Mechanics
```c
typedef struct {
    int x;
    int y;
} Point;

Point p1 = {10, 20};
Point *ptr = &p1;
ptr->x = 15; // Equivalent to (*ptr).x = 15
```

