# 09 — Unions & Enums

## What is this?
- An `enum` defines a set of named integer constants for readable, symbolic code.
- A `union` is a special data structure where all member variables share the **exact same memory location**. The size of a union is the size of its largest member.

## Why does C have it?
- `enum`: Replaces brittle magic numbers with clear state machine indicators.
- `union`: Optimizes memory usage when a value can only be one of several types at any given time (tagged union / variant pattern).

## Syntax and Core Mechanics
```c
typedef enum {
    STATUS_OK = 0,
    STATUS_ERROR = 1,
    STATUS_TIMEOUT = 2
} Status;

typedef union {
    int i_val;
    float f_val;
} DataValue;
```

